\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

We organize results around seven findings (F1--F7) that trace a predictive pathway from trained temporal conditioning to pathological value dynamics. All experiments use Walker Walk (DMC) with DreamerV3; treatments run to 105K steps and the baseline to 505K steps. We report evaluation returns (mean over 10 episodes) and the normalized value-to-eval ratio ($\nve$, \S\ref{sec:metrics}).

%% ============================================================
%% TABLE 1: Main results summary
%% ============================================================

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\caption{Key configurations and outcomes. NVE reports peak $\nve$ in the 35K+ window (\S\ref{sec:metrics}). CIVO-positive seeds are shaded. Complete results for all 43 configurations in Table~\ref{tab:complete_results}.}
\label{tab:main_results}
\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{llcccccl}
\toprule
\textbf{Exp} & \textbf{Config} & \textbf{dt\_emb} & \textbf{Sub} & \textbf{H} & \textbf{Eval@final} & \textbf{NVE} & \textbf{Outcome} \\
\midrule
001 & Baseline ($\dt{=}1$) & --- & --- & 15 & 915.7 @105K & 1.00$\times$ & Healthy \\
\midrule
\rowcolor{gray!15}
008d & Factorial (s1) & trn & ON & 8 & 82.8 @55K & 4.12$\times$ & Full collapse \\
008f & No dt\_emb (s1) & --- & ON & 8 & 503.0 @105K & 0.97$\times$ & Healthy \\
008a & Unexposed emb & exp$^a$ & OFF & 15 & 777.7 @105K & 0.93$\times$ & Healthy \\
015b & Frozen emb & frz$^b$ & ON & 8 & 204.2 @105K & 1.45$\times$ & Grad.\ starvation \\
022b & Frozen emb + Gumbel gate & frz$^b$ & ON & 8 & 300.7 @105K & 0.63$\times$ & No CIVO \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{8}{l}{\textit{Cross-environment validation}} \\
\rowcolor{gray!15}
010b & Cheetah Run & trn & ON & 8 & 51.7 @105K & 8.27$\times$ & Collapsed \\
019b & PickCube-v1 & trn & ON & 8 & 26.64 @200K & 3.78$\times$$^c$ & Transient onset \\
049 & StackCube-v1 & trn & ON & 8 & 34.9 @212K & 5.05$\times$$^d$ & Env.\ transient \\
050 & PegInsertionSide-v1 & trn & ON & 8 & 20.86 @212K & 10.05$\times$$^e$ & Env.\ dynamics$^e$ \\
046 & Cheetah Run & frz$^b$ & ON & 8 & 150.5 @215K & 1.35$\times$ & No CIVO \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\vspace{1pt}
{\raggedright\footnotesize
trn = trained ($d{=}32$, subsample ON);
exp$^a$ = trained, subsample OFF;
frz$^b$ = frozen ($d{=}32$, \texttt{requires\_grad=False});
--- = removed ($d{=}0$).\\
$^c$ NVE normalized against exp\_019a\_v2 (vanilla DreamerV3 on PickCube-v1).\\
$^d$ Frozen control (exp\_051) shows identical spike ($4.30\times$), confirming environment-specific origin.\\
$^e$ Trained/frozen ordering is seed-dependent (environment-specific stochastic dynamics; Table~\ref{tab:cross_domain}).\\
Baselines: Walker 915.7 (row 001); Cheetah ${\sim}320$%% TODO: verify Cheetah baseline—exp_023a 5-seed mean may be ~602.7, not ~320
; PickCube via exp\_019a\_v2; StackCube/PegInsertion not separately measured.\par}
\end{table*}

%% ============================================================
%% F1: All dt>1 degrade
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F1: All Variable-Rate Configurations Degrade}
\label{sec:f1}

Every configuration that introduces $\dt > 1$ imagination degrades relative to the $\dt{=}1$ baseline (Figure~\ref{fig:learning_curves}). The baseline reaches 915.7 at 105K; fixed $\dt{=}2$ (006) peaks at 485.3 before collapsing to 132.9 ($14\%$ of baseline); fixed $\dt{=}4$ (007) never exceeds 176.9 ($19\%$); the learned gate (005) collapses to 98.8 ($11\%$).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.65\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig1_learning_curves.pdf}
\caption{Learning curves. The baseline (black) reaches 915.7; all variable-rate configurations degrade.}
\label{fig:learning_curves}
\end{figure}

%% ============================================================
%% F2: Trained dt_embedding → nve > 1.5x
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F2: Trained Temporal Embedding Precedes Value Overestimation}
\label{sec:f2}

The factorial design isolates trained $\dt$-embedding as the strongest predictor. The \emph{primary} comparison---008d (trained, subsample ON) vs.\ 008f (no embedding, subsample ON)---differs only in embedding training, yielding $\nve = 4.12\times$ (eval 82.8) vs.\ $\nve \leq 0.97\times$ (eval 503.0). \emph{Secondary} comparisons against 008a/008c (unexposed embedding) co-vary subsample status and horizon alongside the embedding factor. The $\nve \geq 1.5\times$ threshold (35K+ window) correctly classifies 27 of 30 experimental configurations across two environments (AUROC${=}0.957$, in-sample; LOEO cross-validated, Table~\ref{tab:nve_loeo}; Figure~\ref{fig:nve_trajectory}).
\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.65\columnwidth]{figures/fig1_nve_trajectory.pdf}
\caption{NVE trajectories (mean $\pm$ seed range). Trained $\dt$-embedding (red, Walker Walk, $n{=}3$ seeds plotted; seeds~4 and~5 data in Table~\ref{tab:complete_results}) exceeds the $1.5\times$ threshold (dashed) in all seeds; no-embedding control (blue) remains near $1.0\times$. Three trajectory types emerge: early collapse (seed~0), late-onset collapse (seed~3), and self-correction (seed~2). Dotted line marks the 35K diagnostic window onset.}
\label{fig:nve_trajectory}
\end{figure}

The trained $\dt$-embedding precedes CIVO in all five Walker Walk seeds (peak NVE $1.53$--$4.72\times$), universally degrading performance to below 30\% of baseline (IQM $148.8$, $n{=}5$; \citealt{agarwal2021precipice}). Combined with Cheetah Run (\S\ref{sec:f6}), CIVO onset is universal across all seven tested seeds ($n{=}7$, two environments) with 6/7 suffering sustained policy collapse, 1/7 self-correcting (Appendix~\ref{sec:seed_divergence}).

%% ============================================================
%% F3--F5: Summary (details in Appendix B)
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F3--F5: Factorial Ablation (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_factorial})}
\label{sec:f3f5_summary}

Trained $\dt$-embedding and multi-rate data exposure are \emph{jointly sufficient in tested configurations} for CIVO. Neither factor alone precedes pathological overestimation: trained embedding without multi-rate exposure (008a, 3 seeds, peak NVE $0.83$--$0.93\times$) and multi-rate exposure without embedding (008f, 3 seeds, peak NVE $\leq 1.41\times$) both remain below $1.5\times$. Cross-environment replication on Cheetah Run confirms generalization. Freezing the embedding (\texttt{requires\_grad=False}) dramatically reduces severity (NVE $0.19$--$1.88\times$ vs.\ trained $1.53$--$4.72\times$; 2/4 seeds below threshold, 2/4 transient self-correcting spikes; Table~\ref{tab:three_way_emb}); removing the embedding (008f) also avoids CIVO. Partial gradient blocking (exp\_044) does not prevent onset (peak NVE $4.45\times$) but permits delayed self-correction (final eval 538, 59\% baseline; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_factorial}), confirming that \emph{any} gradient pathway updating $e_{\dt}$ suffices to induce CIVO. Degradation scales monotonically with $\dt$ ($-47\%$ at $\dt{=}2$, $-81\%$ at $\dt{=}4$; Figure~\ref{fig:dt_amplification}).

\paragraph{ManiSkill manipulation tasks.}
None of three ManiSkill environments exhibit $\dt$-embedding-dependent CIVO. All value-ratio spikes ($4$--$11\times$) are environment-specific: frozen controls show comparable or higher peaks (Table~\ref{tab:cross_domain}), and all self-correct. CIVO is locomotion-specific in DreamerV3; the frozen-embedding diagnostic correctly identifies these transients as non-CIVO.

%% ============================================================
%% F6--F7: Interventions and cross-environment
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F6--F7: Interventions and Multiple Pathways}
\label{sec:f6}

\paragraph{F6: Trained embedding creates multiple vulnerability pathways.}
Exp\_008e\_v3 restricts imagination to $\dt{=}1$ while retaining the trained embedding. Two of four seeds collapse (50\%): Pathway~A (CIVO, $\nve = 4.02\times$) and preliminary Pathway~B (entropy \emph{increase}, $\nve = 3.78\times$; 2 seeds only, requires confirmation; see Figure~\ref{fig:multiple_pathways} in Appendix~\ref{sec:pathways}). Pathway~B produces one of three non-CIVO false positives ($n{=}30$).
\paragraph{F7: Full-pathway gradient stopping shows mixed results.}
Full-SGS (exp\_018) detaches all non-KL gradients from $e_{\dt}$. Seed~1 shows partial mitigation (eval 448.3, NVE $1.66\times$); seed~2 collapses (0/2 elimination; Appendix~\ref{sec:sgs_gradient_analysis}). Cross-environment validation on Cheetah (010b, NVE $8.27$--$108.52\times$) confirms CIVO generalization; full-SGS yields 0/3 elimination. Removing the embedding eliminates CIVO on Cheetah (024b, NVE $< 0.2\times$).

\paragraph{Targeted obs-decoder-only SGS confirms pathway redundancy.}
Obs-decoder-only SGS (exp\_025/026) achieves 1/6 CIVO prevention (1/4 Walker, 0/2 Cheetah; Table~\ref{tab:intervention_hierarchy}), not meaningfully different from full-pathway SGS (0/3). CIVO operates through redundant gradient channels: blocking any single pathway shifts co-adaptation to the remaining ones (Appendix~\ref{sec:seed_divergence}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Intervention hierarchy. Prevention = peak NVE $< 1.5\times$ (35K+). No level reliably eliminates CIVO across environments.}
\label{tab:intervention_hierarchy}
\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\begin{tabular}{llccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Level} & \textbf{Intervention} & \textbf{Seeds} & \textbf{Prev.} & \textbf{Rate} \\
\midrule
--- & Untreated & 7 & 0/7 & 0\% \\
\midrule
Gradient & Reward-only SGS & 2 & 0/2 & 0\% \\
Gradient & Full-pathway SGS & 3 & 0/3 & 0\% \\
Gradient & Obs-decoder SGS & 6 & 1/6 & 17\% \\
\midrule
Output & RAVE ($c{=}1.5{,}2.0$) & 2 & 0/2 & 0\% \\
\midrule
Repr. & TCN ($\sigma{=}0.1$) & 1 & 1/1$^\ddagger$ & 100\% \\
Repr. & TCN ($\sigma{=}0.2$) & 6$^\dagger$ & 2/6 & 33\% \\
Repr. & TCN ($\sigma{=}0.5$) & 3 & 0/3 & 0\% \\
Repr. & TCN ($\sigma{=}1.0$) & 1 & 1/1$^\S$ & 100\% \\
\midrule
Arch. & Wide single & 2 & 0/2 & 0\% \\
Arch. & Narrow dual (min) & 2 & 0/2 & 0\% \\
Arch. & Wide dual (min) & 4 & 3/4$^\|$ & 75\% \\
\midrule
--- & Embedding frozen & 7 & 5/7$^\#$ & 71\% \\
--- & Embedding removed & 5 & 5/5 & 100\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\vspace{1pt}
{\raggedright\footnotesize $^\dagger$Walker 2/4 (s2 NVE $2.11\times$; s3 late-onset CIVO, NVE $3.04\times$ at 105K); Cheetah 0/1, PickCube 0/1.
$^\ddagger$Borderline (NVE${=}1.02\times$, eval 55\%).
$^\S$CIVO prevented but progressive policy erosion (peak 116\%, final 15\% baseline).
$^\|$Walker 3/3 ($\nve \leq 1.4\times$; 1/3 non-CIVO eval collapse), Cheetah 0/1.\\
$^\#$Walker 5 + Cheetah 2. Exp\_047 (NVE $1.71\times$) and Exp\_057 (NVE $1.88\times$): both transient, self-correcting within 20K steps; remaining 5 seeds: NVE $0.19$--$1.45\times$.\par}
\end{table}

%% ============================================================
%% Forward-pass interventions
%% ============================================================

\subsection{Forward-Pass Interventions}
\label{sec:forward_results}

We test two forward-pass alternatives (Table~\ref{tab:intervention_hierarchy}). \textbf{RAVE} (value-level capping) fails: the EMA anchor tracks biased values during CIVO onset. \textbf{TCN} (representation-level noise) suppresses CIVO in 2 of 4 Walker seeds at $\sigma{=}0.2$ (NVE $< 1.0\times$, eval 45--91\%), but remaining seeds show runaway escalation, suggesting TCN gates rather than attenuates CIVO. Cross-environment transfer fails: Cheetah effective dose ($\sigma{=}1.0$) causes policy erosion to 15\% baseline; PickCube 0/1 (Appendix~\ref{sec:tcn_cross_env}). TCN is a \emph{diagnostic probe}, not a deployable mitigation.

%% ============================================================
%% Architecture-Level Ablation
%% ============================================================

\subsection{Architecture-Level Ablation}
\label{sec:arch_results}

\paragraph{Critic convergence in pure imagination.}
Across all dual-critic configurations ($n{=}7$), critics trained on shared RSSM features converge ($\rho > 0.999$), with pessimism at 3--4\% of the independent-critic bound (\S\ref{sec:dual_critic_method}). Pessimistic estimation is thus ineffective; the min operation in wide dual-critic may provide regularization through mechanisms that remain to be characterized (Appendix~\ref{sec:dual_critic_appendix}).

\paragraph{$2{\times}2$ factorial: recovery vs.\ prevention.}
Min drives recovery: narrow dual-critic (${\sim}1.2$M) recovers fully (${\sim}16$--$19\times$ recovery ratio) despite $2\times$ fewer parameters. Prevention requires min $+$ capacity: wide dual-critic maintains $\nve \leq 1.4\times$ (3/3 prevention, 1/3 non-CIVO collapse). Cross-environment transfer is limited (Appendix~\ref{sec:dual_critic_appendix}).

%% ============================================================
%% Online CIVO Detection
%% ============================================================

\subsection{Online CIVO Detection}
\label{sec:detector}

We derive a lightweight online detector monitoring the value-to-evaluation ratio for superlinear growth. Applied to 14 runs across three environments, it achieves TPR${=}5/5$ (95\% Clopper-Pearson CI $[47.8\%, 100\%]$), FPR${=}2/9$ ($22\%$), with predictive alerts in 2/5 cases (Appendix~\ref{sec:detector_appendix}). The detector requires larger-sample validation before deployment as a practical diagnostic tool.

