\subsection{Extended Training Dynamics}\label{sec:extended_training}

One Walker seed (s3, exp\_045) was extended from 112K to approximately 302K steps to investigate long-horizon CIVO dynamics. After onset (NVE peak $4.91\times$ at 132K steps), the system exhibited complete self-recovery: NVE declined to $0.05\times$ and evaluation performance recovered to 867.6 (94.7\% of baseline). This recovery required approximately 170K additional training steps beyond onset. The trajectory shows a single onset--recovery cycle with no recurrence, suggesting that the CIVO feedback loop can break spontaneously given sufficient training---though at substantial computational cost that the frozen $\dt$-embedding diagnostic eliminates entirely.

\subsection{Why MPC Is Immune}
\label{sec:mpc_immune}

\citet{nhu2025tawm} show that $\dt$-conditioning benefits TD-MPC2, raising the question of why it fails in DreamerV3.  The critical difference is that MPC re-plans from real states at every step, so imagination errors do not accumulate into the training signal because there is no critic bootstrapping from imagined trajectories.  In DreamerV3, the actor-critic trains entirely within imagination, and any systematic reward-prediction bias at $\dt > 1$ is amplified through the closed actor-critic loop.

\subsection{The \texorpdfstring{$\dt$}{dt}-Embedding Dilemma}
\label{sec:embedding_dilemma}

The factorial results reveal a fundamental tension: removing the $\dt$-embedding (008f) eliminates CIVO but at a $13\times$ cost to reward prediction quality ($0.7469$ vs.\ $0.0568$ MAE), as the world model cannot distinguish temporal scales.  Restricting imagination to $\dt{=}1$ while retaining the trained embedding (008e\_v3) avoids collapse in 2 of 4 seeds (50\%), while the remaining two collapse via distinct mechanisms: Pathway~A (CIVO-driven, entropy decrease; s3, NVE $4.02\times$) and Pathway~B (entropy-driven, entropy increase; s2, NVE $3.78\times$; \S\ref{sec:f6}).  Neither approach alone provides a complete solution.

\subsection{Multiple Vulnerability Pathways}
\label{sec:pathways}

The two collapse patterns identified in \S\ref{sec:f6}---CIVO (Pathway~A, value-driven, entropy decreasing) and a tentative second mode (Pathway~B, eval-driven, entropy increasing)---have distinct diagnostic signatures. Pathway~B is observed in only two seeds (008e\_v3\_s2 and 038\_s2); we present it as preliminary evidence that the trained $\dt$-embedding's learned features may affect both reward prediction across temporal scales (Pathway~A) and the representation space in which the actor operates (Pathway~B), though additional data is needed to confirm this as a distinct mechanism.  This explains why $\nve$ is sensitive but not perfectly specific: Pathway~B's late evaluation decline inflates $\nve$ through the same arithmetic as CIVO, yielding the sole false positive ($3.78\times$ for 008e\_v3 s2).  Distinguishing the two requires examining the temporal profile and entropy direction of collapse (Figure~\ref{fig:multiple_pathways}).

